{"name":"Cottingley Fairies","hex":"#eddbd7","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fairies","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of fairy","plural of fairie"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fairies"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cottingley Fairies","blocks":[{"text":"The Cottingley Fairies are the subject of a hoax which purports to provide evidence of the existence of fairies. They appear in a series of five photographs taken by Elsie Wright and Frances Griffiths, two young cousins who lived in Cottingley, near Bradford in England. In 1917, when the first two photographs were taken, Elsie was 16 years old and Frances was 9. The pictures came to the attention of writer Sir Arthur Conan Doyle, who used them to illustrate an article on fairies he had been commissioned to write for the Christmas 1920 edition of The Strand Magazine. Doyle was enthusiastic about the photographs, and interpreted them as clear and visible evidence of supernatural phenomena. Public reaction was mixed; some accepted the images as genuine, others believed that they had been faked."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/71/Cottingley_Fairies_1.jpg/330px-Cottingley_Fairies_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cottingley_Fairies"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/71/Cottingley_Fairies_1.jpg/330px-Cottingley_Fairies_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/71/Cottingley_Fairies_1.jpg/330px-Cottingley_Fairies_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The first of the five photographs, taken by Elsie Wright in 1917, shows Frances Griffiths with the alleged fairies.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACottingley_Fairies_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/e